From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Konstantine Zhurakovskiy <konstantine.zhurakovskiy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:21:44 +0700
Subject: upstream-pr-2344: Read marks are aged once they cover a quarter of
 the working set

From seedds/nxnative#2344 (open; commit b5ad4561, the revised form with a cap), taken early for Ocarina of Time's late-session area-load freezes; it replaces 0.28.2's first form. Ported onto upstream's typed read-mark sections: the first scan takes back and folds every section's marks. Drop this patch when nxnative lands #2344.

nvn/pool: age read marks once they cover a quarter of the working set

A read mark (pool_seen) stayed with its ID through every new texture (PR #2022) and was cleared only when the ID was emptied, so the marks grew with every area passed through. An hour into Ocarina of Time (0400486024DEC000, v131072) the pool walk line counted 19,201 of its 33,024 IDs as read (22.7 GB of textures), and a walk makes a marked ID's texture whatever its room (pool_entry, under POOL_REQ_BYTES, 128 MB a frame), so each area load, which streams thousands of textures into IDs earlier areas had read, had them made as fast as the read budget allowed: the loads into Hyrule Field and the passages around it froze in runs of 200-670 ms frames for seconds, with 100-300 first texture uploads a frame and GPU allocated at 9.8 GB against 3.9 GB in the session's first minutes. Another player saw the same on an M4 Pro (24 GB): loads around Kakariko and Lake Hylia at about 5 fps, 17,139 IDs marked (14.2 GB) after 41 minutes (PR #2344).

The host now keeps the size of the marked IDs' textures (seen_size, g_seen_kb_all). While it is above a quarter of Metal's working set (SEEN_CAP_DIV; about 2.7 GB on a 16 GB Mac, 9 GB on a 48 GB one), pool_seen_scan takes the GPU's marks back every 60 frames (SEEN_REARM; an exchange, so a mark set meanwhile stays for the next scan) and notes the frame each mark was last found in (g_seen_at, up to 60 frames after the read); the shaders set them again at their next read (dm_pool_mark is unchanged, so the chain is not). mtl_reg_tex then keeps the mark for the ID's next texture only when the ID was read within the last DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP frames (600 by default): the streamed replacement of a material on screen keeps it as PR #2022 needs, and a texture streamed into an ID left behind holds its stand-in until it is read (pool_stand). Below the cap nothing is taken back or dropped, as before: a mark makes the ID's next texture when it is registered, often hundreds of frames before its first read, and the first form of this change, which aged marks whatever their size, slowed a scripted walk out of Kokiri Forest on an M2 MacBook Air (16 GB) to 70-110 ms frames (GPU 44-60 ms) against 34 ms with DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0 (dpadr's runs, PR #2344). The marks are scanned in a pass of their own before the walk, which may stop at the read budget. A stale 1 read by work still running just after a take-back skips one mark, which a read after the next take-back sets. DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0 keeps every mark until its ID is emptied, as before; the pool stand-ins line counts the marks kept and dropped, with the cap and the marked size. The reference entry (Area loads froze late in a session as read marks piled up) is new, the entry for PR #2022 and Ocarina's note link it.

Checked: t_pool_seen_replace (67 checks, 15 new: a read notes its frame and the ID counts its texture's size; below the cap the GPU's mark stays and an unread ID keeps its mark at its new texture's size; above it the GPU's mark is taken back while the host's stays, a mark not read within the window goes with the next texture, an ID read again keeps its mark; the size goes with the marks; 50 with DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0), t_pool_standin, t_pool_notes, t_pool_chunks, make run in dm/gfx/tests on 713ac08e plus this (every suite passes), test_docs, env_switches.py --check. Two sessions of play on an M5 Pro (48 GB, macOS 26.6.2) with the first form (no cap), not on the same route: without the change, the runs of 200-670 ms frames above after the first hour and tens of 300-frame windows with a p95 above 40 ms; with it, 78 minutes, one such window in 903, GPU allocated 7.2-9.6 GB from the 6th minute on, following the area rather than the time played, and the area loads single frames of 102 and 155 ms (658 and 248 first uploads). 92,703 new textures kept their ID's mark and 46,614 dropped it; stand-ins made 1,235,328 in 6.19 s over the session against about 128,000 without the change (23 us of the submit thread a frame on average). Not run: any game with the cap (neither the long session nor the Kokiri walk), an A/B on one route, regress.py check (no other game here streams into a bindless pool this way), another game, lint_c.py (no LLVM clang-tidy here), iOS. Open: whether a quarter of the working set is the right cap; whether the area-load frames of 100-155 ms left come from the stand-ins made in them or from the first uploads.
Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
