+### Area loads froze late in a session as read marks piled up
+
+- **Symptom:** about an hour into a session, walking into an area or through a passage between areas freezes the picture in runs of 200-670 ms frames for seconds, where the same loads were smooth in the session's first minutes; GPU allocated climbs from 3.9 GB to 9.8 GB, and the `[longframe]` lines show 100-300 first texture uploads a frame. No red line.
+- **Cause:** a read mark (`pool_seen`) stayed with its ID through every new texture ([previous entry](#models-black-for-one-frame-when-a-streaming-step-replaces-their-textures)) and was cleared only when the ID was emptied, so the marks grew with every area the player passed through: an hour in, the `pool walk` line counted 19,201 of 33,024 IDs as read, 22.7 GB of textures. A walk makes a marked ID's texture whatever its room (`pool_entry`, under `POOL_REQ_BYTES`, 128 MB a frame), so an area load, which streams thousands of textures into IDs that earlier areas had read, had them made as fast as the read budget allowed instead of leaving them to stand-ins until a shader read them.
+- **Fix:** once the marks cover more than a quarter of Metal's working set, a mark lasts while its ID is read. The host keeps the size of the marked IDs' textures (`seen_size`, `g_seen_kb_all`); while it is above `recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize / 4` (`SEEN_CAP_DIV`: about 2.7 GB on a 16 GB Mac, 9 GB on a 48 GB one), `pool_seen_scan` takes the GPU's marks back every 60 frames (`SEEN_REARM`; an exchange, so a mark a shader sets meanwhile stays for the next scan), the shaders set them again at their next read (`dm_pool_mark`, unchanged), and the host notes the frame each mark was last found in (`g_seen_at`, up to 60 frames after the read). `mtl_reg_tex` keeps the mark for the ID's next texture only when the ID was read within the last `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP` frames (600 by default, 10 s at 60 fps): the streamed replacement of a material on screen keeps it as before, and a texture streamed into an ID left behind areas ago holds its stand-in until it is read. A stale 1 that work still running reads just after a take-back skips one mark, which a read after the next take-back sets. Below the cap nothing is taken back or dropped, as before: a mark makes the ID's next texture when it is registered, often hundreds of frames before its first read, and aging marks whatever their size slowed an area load on a small Mac (below). `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0` keeps every mark until its ID is emptied, as before. The `pool stand-ins` line counts the marks kept and dropped, with the cap and the marked size.
+- **Check:** `t_pool_seen_replace` (a read notes its frame and the ID counts its texture's size; below the cap the GPU's mark stays and an unread ID keeps its mark; above it the GPU's mark is taken back while the host's stays, a mark not read within the window goes with the next texture, an ID read again keeps its mark; the size goes with the marks), also with `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0`; `t_pool_standin`, `t_pool_notes`, `t_pool_chunks`. Aging with no cap, as first written: on an M2 MacBook Air (16 GB), a scripted Kokiri Forest walk ran 70-110 ms frames (GPU 44-60 ms) at the exit from the village with the default against 34 ms with `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0` (dpadr's runs, PR #2344), which is why the cap is there. Two sessions of play on one Mac (M5 Pro, 48 GB, macOS 26.6.2) with no cap, not on the same route: without the change, the runs of 200-670 ms frames above at the area loads after the first hour, tens of 300-frame windows with a p95 above 40 ms (up to 380 ms) and GPU allocated at 9.8 GB; with it, 78 minutes, one such window in 903, GPU allocated 7.2-9.6 GB from the 6th minute to the end, following the area rather than the time played, and the area loads single frames of 102 and 155 ms (658 and 248 first uploads, 243 and 277 MB). 92,703 new textures kept their ID's mark and 46,614 dropped it. Stand-ins made: 1,235,328 in 6.19 s over the session, against about 128,000 in the session without the change, 23 µs of the submit thread a frame on average. Not run: any game with the cap (neither the long session nor the Kokiri walk), an A/B on one route, another game that streams into a bindless pool, the iPhone. Open: whether a quarter of the working set is the right cap; whether the area-load frames of 100-155 ms left come from the stand-ins made in them or from the first uploads.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (v131072, Hyrule Field and the passages around it).
